# G074 — Online White Board

*v2. See `whiteboard.md` for prose: 2D spatial coordinates replace
1D order with proximity, strokes as first-class retained-mode
entities, z-order is insertion order, spatial queries as linear
scans behind a stable interface, per-author attribution for
collaboration queries.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Collaborative Diagram Session

[diagram-session @room-id
    IN ORDER TO
        support multi-client drawing with per-author undo
        rendered-on-demand SVG export, and broadcast of each change
        so every client converges to the same retained-mode board

    @board <- [whiteboard[new]]
    @room  <- [chat[new-room {id: @room-id}]]

    sync @client-events {
        stroke @client @data ->
            @id <- [whiteboard[add-stroke
                                 {board: @board,
                                  author: @client:name,
                                  color: @data:color,
                                  thickness: @data:thickness,
                                  points: @data:points}]]
            [broadcast[to-all {type: "stroke-added", id: @id}]]

        undo @client ->
            @mine  <- [whiteboard[strokes-by-author
                                    {board: @board,
                                     author: @client:name}]]
            @latest <- @mine last
            @latest
                where exists
                    [whiteboard[remove-stroke
                                  {board: @board, id: @latest:id}]]
                    [broadcast[to-all {type: "stroke-removed",
                                        id: @latest:id}]]

        export ->
            [vault[save
                    {path: "diagram-" + [clock[now]] + ".svg",
                     content: [whiteboard[to-svg
                                            {board: @board,
                                             width: 800, height: 600}]]}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[whiteboard
    new
    add-stroke          {board, author, color, thickness, points}
                                                    ;; -> stroke-id
    remove-stroke       {board, id}                  ;; || not-found
    strokes-by-author   {board, author}
    strokes-near        {board, x, y, radius}
    bounding-box        {board}
    to-svg              {board, width, height}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: proximity replaces order `where` data is 2D

[whiteboard[strokes-near {board: @b, x: @x, y: @y, radius: @r}]
    IN ORDER TO
        find strokes whose points come within @r of (@x, @y)
        using linear scan because the interface is the contract
        and a spatial index is a later optimisation behind it

    @b:strokes
        where any point (px, py) in stroke:points satisfies
              sqrt((px - @x)² + (py - @y)²) ≤ @r
        ordered by insertion-order (same as z-order)
]

Linear scan is correct; quadtree/R-tree/k-d tree replaces the
implementation without changing the interface. Same discipline as
hash-over-flat-array: expose queries, hide the index.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: z-order is insertion order (no z field)

[whiteboard[to-svg {board: @b, ...}]
    @b:strokes walked in insertion order
    emitting <polyline> per stroke in order
    where first stroke is underneath, last stroke is on top
    IN ORDER TO
        keep z as storage-order so undo (remove middle stroke)
        does not renumber anything the surviving strokes depend on
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @b  <- [whiteboard[new]]
    @s1 <- [whiteboard[add-stroke
                         {board: @b, author: "alice",
                          color: "black", thickness: 2,
                          points: [{x:10, y:10}, {x:50, y:50}]}]]
    @s2 <- [whiteboard[add-stroke
                         {board: @b, author: "bob",
                          color: "red", thickness: 2,
                          points: [{x:40, y:40}, {x:80, y:80}]}]]

    -> [whiteboard[strokes-by-author {board: @b, author: "alice"}]]
    ;; -> [stroke @s1]

    -> [whiteboard[strokes-near
                     {board: @b, x: 45, y: 45, radius: 10}]]
    ;; -> [stroke @s1, stroke @s2]  (both pass near (45,45))

    -> [whiteboard[bounding-box {board: @b}]]
    ;; -> {min-x: 10, min-y: 10, max-x: 80, max-y: 80}
]
